</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Ineffectively tested methods represent the minority class and are therefore more difficult to predict.
Table <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 5.5. Results ‣ 5. Empirical Study ‣ Is the Stack Distance Between Test Case and Method Correlated With Test Effectiveness?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows the within-project prediction performance
for identifying ineffectively tested methods.
Median precision of this outcome is 70.7% and median recall is 34.3%.
In the best case, 96.6% precision and 100.0% recall are still achieved (<span id="S5.SS5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Commons Stat</span>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="1903.05432v1/varImp_MXX_JFCH_detected.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>RQ 2: Variable importance of <span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">JFreechart</span>’s prediction model (scaled to one).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.5. Results ‣ 5. Empirical Study ‣ Is the Stack Distance Between Test Case and Method Correlated With Test Effectiveness?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> exemplary presents the variable importance
of <span id="S5.SS5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">JFreechart</span>’s within-project prediction model.
The figure shows that the minimal stack distance and the minimal scope value of a method’s covering test cases
(the scope of a test case expresses how many methods it covers)
are the most important variables for the prediction model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-project prediction for identifying ineffectively tested methods only achieves a poor performance.
Even when applying the over-sampling technique <span id="S5.SS5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SMOTE<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">6</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Synthetic Minority Over-Sampling Technique </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="footnote6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">(</span><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chawla
et al., 2002</a><span id="footnote6.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">)</span></cite></span></span></span></span> to pre-process training sets,
median precision is only 19.2% and median recall is 43.2%.
Hence, cross-project prediction is not well suited for uncovering ineffectively tested methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p13.1" class="ltx_p">
<span id="S5.SS5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="S5.SS5.p13.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">The mutation testing result of a method can on average be predicted
with 92.9% precision and 93.4% recall.
Cross-project prediction is more challenging and achieves a weaker performance.</span>
</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8. </span>RQ 2: Performance when predicting the mutation result <span id="S5.T8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of a method test-case pair.</span></figcaption>
